{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:19630402 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:19630402 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"19630402","source":"MED","pmid":"19630402","pmcid":"PMC2889180","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2889180"]},"doi":"10.1021/jm900277z","title":"Identification of enoxacin as an inhibitor of osteoclast formation and bone resorption by structure-based virtual screening.","authorString":"Ostrov DA, Magis AT, Wronski TJ, Chan EK, Toro EJ, Donatelli RE, Sajek K, Haroun IN, Nagib MI, Piedrahita A, Harris A, Holliday LS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ostrov DA","lastName":"Ostrov","firstName":"David A","initials":"DA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Immunology and Laboratory Medicine, University of Florida College of Medicine, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Magis AT","lastName":"Magis","firstName":"Andrew T","initials":"AT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0576-677X"}},{"fullName":"Wronski TJ","lastName":"Wronski","firstName":"Thomas J","initials":"TJ"},{"fullName":"Chan EK","lastName":"Chan","firstName":"Edward K L","initials":"EK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3938-9503"}},{"fullName":"Toro EJ","lastName":"Toro","firstName":"Edgardo J","initials":"EJ"},{"fullName":"Donatelli RE","lastName":"Donatelli","firstName":"Richard E","initials":"RE"},{"fullName":"Sajek K","lastName":"Sajek","firstName":"Kristen","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Haroun IN","lastName":"Haroun","firstName":"Ireni N","initials":"IN"},{"fullName":"Nagib MI","lastName":"Nagib","firstName":"Michael I","initials":"MI"},{"fullName":"Piedrahita A","lastName":"Piedrahita","firstName":"Ana","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Harris A","lastName":"Harris","firstName":"Ashley","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Holliday LS","lastName":"Holliday","firstName":"L Shannon","initials":"LS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0844-1965"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0844-1965"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0576-677X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3938-9503"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"16","volume":"52","journalIssueId":1661970,"dateOfPublication":"2009 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2009,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Chem","title":"Journal of medicinal chemistry","essn":"1520-4804","issn":"0022-2623","nlmid":"9716531","isoabbreviation":"J Med Chem"},"printPublicationDate":"2009-08-01"},"pubYear":"2009","pageInfo":"5144-5151","abstractText":"An interaction between the B2 subunit of vacuolar H(+)-ATPase (V-ATPase) and microfilaments is required for osteoclast bone resorption. An atomic homology model of the actin binding site on B2 was generated and molecular docking simulations were performed. Enoxacin, a fluoroquinolone antibiotic, was identified and in vitro testing demonstrated that enoxacin blocked binding between purified B2 and microfilaments. Enoxacin dose dependently reduced the number of osteoclasts differentiating in mouse marrow cultures stimulated with 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D(3), as well as markers of osteoclast activity, and the number of resorption lacunae formed on bone slices. Enoxacin inhibited osteoclast formation at concentrations where osteoblast formation was not altered. In summary, enoxacin is a novel small molecule inhibitor of osteoclast bone resorption that acts by an unique mechanism and is therefore an attractive lead molecule for the development of a new class of antiosteoclastic agents.","affiliation":"Department of Pathology, Immunology and Laboratory Medicine, University of Florida College of Medicine, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","r